1 | INTRODUCTION
Data-intensive research continues to expand across all fields of medicine with the goal of improving healthcare delivery, clinical decision-making,
and patient outcomes. There is a growing need in academic health centres to recruit and train quantitative scientists who can effectively
collaborate with clinical and translational investigators on data-intensive research (Institute of Medicine, 2004). Quantitative scientists can have a
diverse set of formal training and may identify as biostatisticians, informaticists, data scientists, psychometricians, bioinformaticians, epidemiolo
gists, implementation scientists, and/or engineers. To meet the needs of a data-rich research environment, many academic health centres have
developed “Quantitative Science Units” that house these scientists in a centralized entity that coordinates collaboration between quantitative
experts and domain scientists (Hanlon et al., 2022). Recent literature has established strong arguments for Domain Science Units to engage with
Quantitative Science Units rather than hiring quantitative scientists on their own (Desai et al., 2022; Welty et al., 2013). Quantitative Science
Units differ from consulting or service units that focus on fee-for-service work or contracts on a per-project basis. Instead, they facilitate the
creation of teams of quantitative scientists (“Quantitative Science Teams”) to partner with specific research labs, centres, departments, divisions,
schools, and so forth (“Domain Science Units”). Quantitative Science Units support the infrastructure of collaborative research by supporting the
practice of quantitative science, professional development of quantitative scientists, reproducible and rigorous quantitative research, and
innovative methodology and analytic tool development.
The development and management of long-term partnerships between Quantitative Science Units and Domain Science Units is key to
developing sustainable infrastructure for translational research. Establishing and maintaining partnerships comes with unique challenges that
require specialized structure and processes. Existing literature has described some best practices related to organizational structure, staffing,
benefits, and funding models of Quantitative Science Units (Desai et al., 2022; Hanlon et al., 2022; Ittenbach & DeAngelis, 2012; Lesser &
Parker, 1995). Basic guidance has been published for biostatisticians to estimate effort required to engage in individual research projects
(Lesser & Parker, 1995) and on defining consensus-driven evaluation metrics for quantitative collaborations (Rubio et al., 2011). However, current
literature lacks guidance on principles for the development, management, and evaluation of the Quantitative Science Teams (hereon referred to
as “Teams”) that partner with individual Domain Science Units.
To develop guidance and best practices that can be used by other Quantitative Science Units, especially newly forming units, leaders of
Quantitative Science Units across six institutions formed a working group. Table 1 describes the units, which range from larger groups with over
20 partnerships to a unit with three partnerships that is actively working to expand. Importantly, partnerships are highly variable, with some
partnerships in one institution representing engagement with over 500 faculty, whereas some partnerships in another institution involve the lab
of one investigator. The primary objective of this work is to provide a flexible, comprehensive framework for Quantitative Science Units to
develop sustainable, long-term relationships with Domain Science Units. The outline of the paper is as follows: (1) We provide guidance on how
to identify the need for a new partnership, tools for assessing the effort and expertise needed to support the partnership, and examples of
