2 | DEVELOPING A COLLABORATIVE PARTNERSHIP
A “collaborative partnership” is defined as a relationship between a Quantitative Science Unit and a Domain Science Unit, in which the Quantita
tive Science Unit designates a team of quantitative scientists who collaborate with investigators in the Domain Science Unit on an ongoing basis.
Figure 1 illustrates how a Team supports a collaborative partnership. Such partnerships foster numerous individual collaborations between quanti
tative scientists and individual investigators within the Domain Science Unit. Quantitative Science Units may engage in partnerships with Domain
Science Units of varying size, encompassing large department- or division-wide analytic teams to smaller teams that collaborate with a single lab
or investigator. Identification of new partnerships may occur in multiple ways. In many cases, investigator(s) from a Domain Science Unit approach
the Quantitative Science Unit for help identifying potential quantitative collaborators. The Quantitative Science Unit may identify the need to
develop a partnership through repeated requests from investigators within a Domain Science Unit. Quantitative Science Unit leaders may also
conduct systematic outreach meetings with the Chairs of Departments and Divisions to promote the benefits of an ongoing partnership.
Before entering a partnership, leadership of the Quantitative Science and Domain Science Units should discuss research and funding goals of
the Domain Science Unit, rigour and reproducibility practices, and team science principles to ensure fitness and feasibility of a partnership. Once
both parties agree that a collaborative partnership would be mutually beneficial, there are three key elements to establishing a formal partnership:
(1) performing a needs assessment to identify all of the appropriate expertise needed (e.g., biostatistics, epidemiology, and bioinformatics),
(2) defining the level of staff/faculty effort, equipment, and materials required, and (3) documenting and defining the financial terms and the scope
of the collaboration. A checklist that summarizes these elements is provided in Section 1 of Table 2.
2.1 | Perform a needs assessment for the Domain Science Unit
The purpose of a needs assessment is to understand the research landscape of the Domain Science Unit and how quantitative scientists can con
tribute to data-intensive research projects. The needs assessment should evaluate current and upcoming research activities that quantitative sci
entists may be involved in over the next 6 to 12 months. This information can be gleaned through meetings between the Quantitative Science
Unit leadership, potential Quantitative Science Team members, and individual investigators from the Domain Science Unit. Deploying a survey
can also help leadership determine the required expertise and amount of ongoing effort that should be allocated to the Quantitative Science
Team. Appendix S1 provides a table of common information solicited from Domain Science Units at the individual project level in order to assess
the volume and nature of projects. Appendix S2 provides an example survey used by Duke which often assesses Domain Science Unit needs at a
more aggregate level (i.e., at the level of investigator or Domain Science Unit). The survey includes questions such as “How many projects do you
have that require biostatistics collaboration in the next 3–6 months?,” to be answered by leadership in a Domain Science Unit.
2.2 | Define effort and roles for the Quantitative Science Team (and Domain Science Unit)
The leadership of the Quantitative Science Unit can use the results of the needs assessment to estimate the appropriate effort allocations for the
Quantitative Science Team. The overall staff and faculty effort proposed for the team depends on the volume and variety of project types, as well
as the amount and type of funding available for analytic work. Broad guidelines that have been published for estimating effort at the individual
project level can help the Quantitative Science Unit estimate the effort needed (Lesser & Parker, 1995; Peterson et al., 2022). The effort
estimation is a task that should be completed by the Quantitative Science Unit leadership and ideally the quantitative scientists who would be on
the Team. The initial estimate may then be negotiated with the Domain Science Unit leadership with a more in-depth discussion about available
budget and scope of work. Table 3 provides examples of what effort estimates might look like for a relatively limited effort model (e.g., 40% effort
from a staff biostatistician and 15% effort of a faculty biostatistician) as well as a larger team that has multiple staff and/or faculty working full
time on the team. This exhibits that both internal and external funding can be used to support numerous quantitative scientists who coordinate
among each other to work on a variety of projects in the Domain Science Unit.
It is also crucial to include funding for project management (sometimes termed “administrative”) activities such as tracking project status,
developing reports about productivity, conflict resolution, meeting scheduling, and other relevant tasks. In our experiences, the most successful
Teams dedicate time to project management. Appendix S3 provides an example guidance document from the Duke Biostatistics, Epidemiology,
