Similar to Duke, Stanford's Quantitative Sciences Unit earmarks specific dedicated effort for their senior-level quantitative scientists (faculty
and/or senior staff) to act as project managers. In newer units such as the University of Kentucky Biostat CIRCL, a single senior-level quantitative
scientist is typically identified to lead each partnership. That individual engages with junior-level quantitative scientists as needed for each project.
A similar model is followed at Colorado's Center for Innovative Design and Analysis (CIDA). The most successful Teams, in our experience,
generally have a minimum of one PhD and one Master's level team member. At some institutions, the individuals participating in the collaborative
partnership may be somewhat fluid—even within the predefined scope and effort allocation to the partnership—in order to accommodate for
unexpected expertise needs that occur over time.
2.3 | Document and define the administrative, financial terms, and scope of the partnership
To ensure transparency and shared expectations, an internal governing document (e.g., memorandum of understanding or MOU) that summarizes
mutual expectations between the Quantitative Science Unit and the Domain Science Unit should be drafted. An important element of the
document is to affirm a commitment from the Domain Science Unit to guarantee funding for the effort of the Quantitative Science Team for a set
period of time. The Quantitative Science Unit Leadership must also assess the extent to which the needs and expectations align with the available
budget of the Domain Science Unit. It may be helpful for the Quantitative Science Unit Lead to work with the financial administrator of the
Quantitative Science Unit to establish the minimums they expect. Some essential components of these agreements include administrative and
financial contacts, the start and end date of the agreement (and renewal clause), effort level in full time equivalence of Quantitative Science
Team members, any associated fees, fund codes associated with the funding sources, and expectations about when and how funding will be
processed. Agreements should establish the level of quantitative scientist effort retained in perpetuity. It can be challenging for Domain Science
Units to identify enough funding to sufficiently cover the appropriate level of effort from the Team, especially for pre-award work, so it is
important to have institutional support and plan in advance. Financial commitments should incorporate the costs for training, professional
development, management, computing resources, and other related expenses. Depending on funding sources, the financial component of the
agreements should be flexible to accommodate expanding scopes of work or new funding streams, such as an influx of grant funding for
research projects.
The governing document may also define the structure and scope of work of the Quantitative Science Team. Appendix S4 provides example
agreements that function as governing documents for Quantitative Science Units at four different institutions. While some Quantitative Science
Units only outline the financial terms in an MOU (see Example 1 in Appendix S4), others may include details on the mutual expectations of each
party (see Example 2 in Appendix S4). Some Quantitative Science Units may include additional details, including naming specific personnel that
will be a part of the Quantitative Science Team (see Example 3 in Appendix S4). More detailed MOUs might further outline specific expectations
regarding response times for intake requests, reporting milestones and frequency, and expectations regarding faculty recruitment (see Example
4 in Appendix S4). The governing document should be periodically revisited and potentially amended to accommodate changes in the scope of
work, amount of funding available, shifting Domain Science Unit goals, and bilateral expectations. These documents help ensure that each party
enters the partnership with aligned expectations and mutual accountability. The documents provide a shared understanding, which allows the
opportunity to discuss details and avoid misunderstanding. It is important to note that, in practice, there may be collaborations that occur outside
of the context of an MOU. In such cases, Quantitative Science Unit Leads may decide on a case-by-case basis whether a formal document is
needed.
3 | IMPLEMENTATION AND MANAGEMENT OF A QUANTITATIVE SCIENCE TEAM
Once a Quantitative Science Team is established, processes should be defined to ensure the success, longevity, and growth of the partnership.
Ongoing management involves the development, maintenance, and refinement of processes that help the Team function effectively. This
section is designed to provide tools and information to help a Quantitative Science Team (1) define collaboration goals and metrics of success;
(2) establish processes for project intake, review, and approval; (3) set rules for prioritization of project requests; and (4) track projects and monitor
effort. A checklist that summarizes these elements is provided in Section 2 of Table 2.
