3.1 | Define collaboration goals and metrics of success
The Quantitative Science Team must work closely with the Domain Science Unit Liaison to assess and document short- and long-term goals and
metrics that would demonstrate success. Appendix S5 offers a discussion guide to define goals with a Domain Science Unit Liaison such as “What
outcomes/metrics would demonstrate that you are accomplishing your short-term goals?” Metrics for success may vary substantially depending
on the priorities of the partnership. Rubio et al. (2011) establishes a framework for categorizing the types of metrics that may be used to evaluate
the success of different types of collaborative activities, providing example metrics such as “grants submitted” and “research faculty supported.”
Goals and metrics should be discussed annually in order to create the opportunity for both sides to evaluate success, update the goals, and review
potential barriers and facilitators of success.
3.2 | Define process for project intake and review
A transparent process for investigators in the Domain Science Unit to request collaboration with the Quantitative Science Team should be
developed. Establishing this “project intake process” enables the Quantitative Science Team and Domain Science Unit Liaison to review and
assess scope of work in order to plan, navigate, and prioritize requests. A secondary goal is to facilitate tracking outcomes of the partnership to
measure success and assess unmet needs.
The information included in an intake form should be thoughtfully chosen. At a minimum, intake forms typically include contact information
of the investigator, study description (title, brief background, research questions, scope of work, and goals), and deadlines. The level of detail
collected during the intake process can vary, ranging from a short form that takes the investigators 2 min to complete to a more elaborate process
that requires an aims page and a two-page proposal. The intake process should seek to strike a balance between collecting enough pertinent
information without overwhelming investigators and potentially deterring them from submitting a request. An example of a brief intake form can
be found in Appendix S6. A more detailed intake form designed for Duke investigators to find potential quantitative collaborators was recently
published and could also be used as a reference for those building such a form (Pomann et al., 2023).
3.3 | Prioritization of submitted project requests
Once the intake process is established, it is important to develop a process for prioritizing and assigning the projects. The process relies on several
factors, such as Domain Science Unit goals and priorities, the available bandwidth of the Quantitative Science Team, and project deadlines. Before
accepting a project, the Team Lead will likely need to meet with the submitting investigator to understand the scientific goals, general scope of
work, and deadlines. Revision of the project scope of work may be needed to align with available data or to meet a deadline. Reasons for declining
a project request should be agreed upon with the Domain Science Unit Liaison and tracked within the intake system.
