When accepted projects outweigh the available time of the Quantitative Science Team (i.e., demand for collaboration exceeds supply), we
recommend that Domain Science Unit Liaison(s) determine the priority of the projects being proposed. For example, if the agreed-upon goal of
the partnership is to increase external funding, the Domain Science Unit might prioritize collaboration on grant proposals over a conference
abstract. Alternatively, if the primary goal is to provide mentorship for early-career investigators and increase publication of internally funded
research projects, the Domain Science Unit may prioritize the conference abstract. Appendix S7 provides a detailed template, used by the Duke
BERD Core, that can be adapted to document the process for how individual projects are requested, assigned, and prioritized in a Quantitative
Science Team.
3.4 | Ongoing project tracking, effort monitoring, and effort projection
After projects are assigned, the Quantitative Science Team needs to track progress and effort spent on each project so they can evaluate
productivity and ensure project goals are met. The process should be user-friendly, allowing for easy input and extraction of information, and
should allow for reports to be easily generated for evaluation of a Quantitative Science Team's success. While it is ideal to integrate project intake,
project tracking, effort monitoring, and metric evaluation, the authors have not found any commercial products that could serve all of these needs
at once. An effective project tracking system will collect a comprehensive list of projects and investigators working with the Quantitative Science
Team, including pertinent information about goals and progress. Quantitative Science Units at Duke, Stanford, Virginia Tech, the University of
Kentucky, and Henry Ford Health use a Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) database that links the intake form with additional data entry
forms on the back end, allowing collaboration staff to track milestones and project outputs (Harris et al., 2009, 2019). In each case, data are
extracted from the database and summarized for reporting. A data dictionary for the Duke BERD Core's project tracking database is included in
Appendix S8. Colorado's CIDA Unit developed its own Smartsheet-based system. CIDA's system includes team dashboards that are updated live.
This allows for the Quantitative Science Unit and Domain Science Unit to continuously evaluate overall goals, individual project progress, demand
for the Unit, and fiscal expenses.
Monitoring approximate effort allocated to each assigned project, on a monthly basis, allows for transparency with the Domain Science Unit
and can help facilitate trust building within the Partnership. It also helps the Quantitative Science Team to plan for new projects and communicate
with financial personnel responsible for managing the funding. Academic institutions have varying ways of tracking effort on specific fund codes,
but the goal in this context is specific to project management and planning. In Appendix S9, we provide an example of how effort can be tracked.
Maintaining an up-to-date effort tracking process involves all members of the Team entering and updating their allocations.
It is essential for the Team to update project and effort tracking systems. Emphasizing the utility of the collected data supports buy-in;
however, it is also crucial that the devised system is simple, easy to understand, and straightforward to maintain. Ideally, both the project and
effort tracking systems should allow for simultaneous editing, such as through a tool like MS OneNote, Smartsheet, a data entry form, or an online
database. For best engagement, the process may need to be iteratively designed and refined over time. Establishing a pilot period during which a
new tool is tested and evaluated may be helpful in building management processes that serve the needs of the partnership. Likewise, new systems
implemented at the level of the Quantitative Science Unit can be piloted and refined with one Team first before being deployed more widely.
Updating the system could be the responsibility of the Quantitative Science Team members or assistance could be provided by someone in a
more administrative role.
4 | EVALUATION OF COLLABORATIVE PARTNERSHIPS
Measuring the value that the Quantitative Science Team contributes to the Domain Science Unit can be challenging and should not be assumed
to be self-evident. Both the Quantitative Science Unit and the Domain Science Unit should annually evaluate the satisfaction of Team members
and investigators as well as success in accomplishing short- and long-term goals from the previous year. If the collaboration is new, it is advisable
to start evaluation meetings monthly and then to reduce the cadence as the process becomes more routine. Challenges related to data access or
quality, efficiency, interpersonal communication with investigators, or project prioritization should be documented and communicated to address
impediments to quality research. Methods of project tracking and evaluation should provide “early warning systems” to identify and resolve
challenges. Team members may exercise judgement to determine when collaboration challenges should be escalated to Domain Science Unit
Liaison(s) and/or the leadership of the Quantitative Science Unit.
Tracking helps the Team assess the types of projects that occurred in the past year, align output with effort allocations, and anticipate future
research and staffing trends. Evaluation of the data can help identify barriers to achieving goals, identify inefficiencies with processes, and
recalibrate project prioritization guidelines. The following sections provide tools and guidance to help Teams evaluate (1) the satisfaction of Team
members and collaborators in the Domain Science Unit and (2) how well the Quantitative Science Team was able to reach the predefined goals. A
checklist that summarizes the essential elements of evaluation of Partnerships is provided in Section 3 of Table 2.
