4.1 | Evaluation of collaboration experience and satisfaction
When evaluating a partnership, the goal should be to assess whether Team members are interacting productively and to identify areas for
improvement that can contribute to more successful and efficient research collaborations. At Stanford, the Quantitative Sciences Unit captures
project-specific progress and Team performance via regular automated surveys to both the Domain Science Unit investigators and the assigned
Quantitative Science Team members. Both parties are asked feedback questions regarding the nature of the collaborative engagement at different
stages of the project timeline (60 days from inception, 365 days, and so forth). Discordant responses between the Quantitative Science Team
members and the Domain Science Unit investigators can prompt additional investigation into the disconnect to resolve issues with Team
dynamics. Appendix S10 provides the surveys for the Team member and the Domain Science Unit collaborator after 60 and 365 days from pro
ject initiation. The Domain Science Unit Liaison may seek additional qualitative feedback from individual investigators to help identify successes
and challenges encountered by the investigators in accessing the resource or collaborating with the Team. The Duke BERD Core combines the
collaboration evaluation processes with annual staff performance evaluation through the use of annual collaborator surveys. Appendix S11
provides Duke's Annual Collaborator survey sent to each investigator that a staff member has collaborated with over the past year to evaluate
the quality of the staff's communication, statistical, and programming skills. A similar annual mutual feedback process is used at Colorado's CIDA.
Feedback received from investigators serves as a satisfaction indicator and helps guide professional development goals for the staff member for
the following year.
Teams should also evaluate how well Team members support one another and the effectiveness of the procedures they implement to
increase efficiency. Teams can implement guided “bidirectional” feedback activities to encourage members to evaluate one another in a way that
improves teamwork. Appendix S12 provides a question guide used by the Duke BERD Core for bidirectional feedback sessions, for example,
asking each party, “What could I have done better in the last year to help you reach your goals?” This activity should be a forum for clear and
honest communication, providing the opportunity for both positive and constructive feedback and a chance to strategize ways to enhance
working relationships between Team members. At Virginia Tech, a newer Quantitative Science Unit, a unit-wide, team-based approach is used
wherein the entire unit meets weekly to evaluate current ongoing projects. Based on workloads and deadlines, Teams can then be enhanced or
reduced as necessary, with minimal disruption. Additionally, weekly project-based meetings take place with Quantitative Science Unit leadership
to refine and update project lists. This helps ensure that all Team members are provided weekly priority lists, facilitates communication and
transparency, and draws attention to imbalances in workload and looming deadlines.
4.2 | Evaluation of goals and metrics
It can be beneficial to evaluate progress and productivity on a monthly or quarterly basis. Leadership of the Quantitative Science Unit and the
Domain Science Unit should meet together with the Quantitative Science Team at least annually to review and evaluate metrics of interest. An
example quarterly report used by the Duke BERD Core to aid this discussion is provided in Appendix S13. This report includes commonly used
metrics but can be further tailored to the specific goals of a collaboration. For example, if increasing external funding is a goal, the report might list
the number of grants submitted and awarded.
The Domain Science Unit, in partnership with the Team, should assess whether the reported metrics align well with the partnership goals. A
template for discussing goals and outcomes, along with accelerators and barriers, is included in Appendix S14. A goal related to the funding model
should be discussed annually to determine whether funding allocations should be revisited. For example, one funding-related goal could be “Use
the 20% departmental funding to collaborate with investigators to submit at least 6 proposals for external funding that will each include at least
10% effort for a quantitative scientist.”
5 | DISCUSSION
This paper outlines specific strategies and targeted recommendations for developing, managing, and evaluating Quantitative Science Teams in
academic health centres. The guidance provided here is intended for Quantitative Science Units to establish long-term partnerships with Domain
Science Units, as opposed to shorter term fee-for-service arrangements. We have found that the methods presented in this paper help to mitigate
challenges that we have experienced such as misunderstandings on goals, expectations, and deadlines. In addition to supporting long-term
partnerships, many Quantitative Science Units provide office hours, pursue educational initiatives, engage in short-term collaborations, and
present seminars, among other activities. It is important to emphasize that there is no “one-size-fits-all” approach that will work well for every unit
or even every Team within a unit. We hope these tools and guidance from our diverse units will provide options for other units to adopt and
adapt without substantial initial effort investment.
