It is crucial to emphasize the importance of dedicating time to developing processes that overcome common barriers such as a lack of shared
vision, communication challenges, conflicting goals, unclear roles, and lack of trust between collaborating partners. The relationship between the
Quantitative Science Unit and the Domain Science Unit must be bidirectional, involving effective communication and mutual support. Teams must
also develop processes for early issue identification and communication. While problems can typically be resolved collaboratively with Domain
Science Unit Liaison(s), access to supportive leadership within the Quantitative Science Unit, Domain Science Unit, and of the Academic
Institution is essential.
Each of the Quantitative Science Units discussed here started with limited staff and a small number of partnerships. Ultimately, all units grew
to a size where having tools and processes to collect administrative data became a critical driver of success. For example, over 5 years, the Center
for Innovative Design and Analysis at the University of Colorado grew from six collaborative partnerships to 27, with continued growth. Some of
their scientific successes included supporting COVID-19 research that formed real-world policy around monoclonal antibody usage (Aggarwal
et al., 2022; Douin et al., 2022). They have also been successful in obtaining R01 funding from NIH with coprimary investigators in both the
Quantitative Science and Domain Science Units.
To grow in a sustainable way, it is crucial that Quantitative Science Units budget for the administrative time and effort required of all
Team members as they implement new processes and tracking. Long-term partnerships represent a dedicated commitment and investment in
research infrastructure and are critical for enabling the growth of the Quantitative Sciences Units necessary to accommodate academic health
centres' data-related needs for conducting translational research. For Quantitative Science Units that historically supported short-term
engagements but are looking to shift towards long-term partnerships, implementing an intake form for project tracking may help support
developing collaborative partnerships. This task will require administrative time to complete. Charging partnership fees can help the
Quantitative Science Unit pay for administrative costs and should be considered in the Partnership budgeting stage. Academic health centres
may consider providing institutional funding for this instead of charging fees to each partnering Domain Science Unit. For many of the units
described in this manuscript, administrative effort is covered by a mixture of funding that includes some portion of institutional support.
Having Teams work closely with the Quantitative Science Unit and its fiscal leaders ensures the most critical components are properly
funded and structured.
Protocols and tools presented here evolved over the course of several years and numerous collaborations. In that time, our Quantitative
Science Units and individual teams employed some strategies that proved to be inefficient or otherwise flawed. For example, many Quantitative
Science Teams in Duke BERD originally used simple intake forms and separately tracked project progress in MS Excel spreadsheets for each
partnership. This process proved inefficient due to the amount of administrative time needed to maintain tracking and extract metrics for
reporting. This led to the creation of intake forms that link to project tracking information using REDCap software. The intake and tracking tool
subsequently went through significant refinement over the course of 2 years, which involved collecting feedback from users, to ensure that that
the tracker was capturing the most meaningful and appropriate metrics. Allowing time for development and evaluation led to a more efficient
process for prioritizing projects and generating automated reports to evaluate partnerships. Finding ways that our tools could serve multiple
purposes has been very beneficial for evaluating productivity and understanding overall needs. At the same time, another key lesson learned has
been to avoid overharmonization. As more partnerships are taken on, we have learned that trying to make tools overly consistent across all
Quantitative Science Teams was too limiting for some Teams. Providing tools and templates for each team but allowing for adaptation of
processes to the specific goals of each Partnership has been critical in better meeting the needs of all groups. Though we have provided tools that
work well for our Quantitative Science Units, there is a continued need to address new challenges as they arise. We recommend that similar Units
(and Teams) that make use of these tools first pilot and evaluate them and then refine and adapt them to the goals of their partnerships to
maximize their usefulness.
